<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Theodoros Negris</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1790">1790</a>–<a href="/years/1824">1824</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Theodoros Negris (1790–1824) was a Greek scholar and diplomat from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> who served the Ottoman administration before joining the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-war-of-independence">Greek War of Independence</a>. In 1821 he drew up the Legal Order of Eastern Continental <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and led its regional government, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/areopagus">Areopagus</a>, and he was the first Greek secretary for foreign affairs. He died of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/typhus">typhus</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nafplio">Nafplio</a> in 1824.</p></div>
